[154.163, "o", "\u001b[0m\u2699 \u001b[0mard-google-discovery_ard_search {\"query\":\"query an enterprise BigQuery dataset\"}\r\n"]
[156.358, "o", "The best tool is the **Google BigQuery Managed MCP Server** (`urn:ai:google.com:mcp:bigquery`) \u2014 it executes SQL queries, inspects table schemas, and runs dry-runs with\r\n"]
[156.404, "o", "\u001b[0m\u2699 \u001b[0mard-google-discovery_ard_auth_status Unknown\r\n"]
[158.904, "o", "The **Google BigQuery Managed MCP Server** (`urn:ai:google.com:mcp:bigquery`) is the right tool \u2014 it executes SQL queries, inspects table schemas, explores datasets, and supports query dry-runs with cost estimation.\r\n\r\nIt requires GCP credentials (Tier 3), which your environment doesn't currently have. To use it, you'd need to onboard:\r\n\r\n1. Contact your enterprise admin to grant BigQuery access to your GCP account\r\n2. Run `gcloud auth application-default login` on your terminal\r\n\r\nAlternatively, there's the **BigQuery SQL & Performance Guidelines Skill** (`tier:0`, no auth) for SQL optimization, partitioning, and window functions when the MCP server isn't available.\r\n\r\nWant to onboard the BigQuery MCP server via Application Default Credentials? If so, I'll persist your consent and hand you the exact commands to run.\r\n"]
[160.631, "o", "\u001b[?2004hdeveloper@workstation:~/ard-project$ "]
